---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = "/use-cases/holiday-gifting";
const title = "Shopify holiday gifting email: intent, deadlines and handoff";
const desc = "For the campaign owner coordinating gift discovery with fulfillment. A useful gifting sequence helps the buyer choose, explains the available delivery options and preserves the distinction between purchaser and recipient.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Is a separate shipping name enough to classify a gift?",
    "answer": "No. Preserve uncertainty and prefer an explicit gift choice associated with the relevant order or line."
  },
  {
    "question": "When should shipping reminders send?",
    "answer": "Use the store\u2019s current fulfillment-approved cutoff register and recipient destination; this page provides no universal schedule."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should gift purchasers receive care instructions?",
    "answer": "Offer shareable instructions and account for mixed baskets. The purchaser may need to pass those details to the recipient."
  },
  {
    "question": "What happens after physical shipping closes?",
    "answer": "Remove unsupported delivery promises and show only verified alternatives with their own conditions."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <article class="container prose-shopify py-16">
    <a class="link" href="/use-cases">All use cases</a>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 text-xl">{desc}</p>
    <section class="py-6"><h2>Keep gift intent specific</h2><p>Store an explicit gift choice with its order or line item when available. A different shipping name or a gift-guide click is a clue, not proof that every future purchase is a gift. Keep unknown intent as a valid state. A mixed basket may need both gift and self-use handling; do not suppress all product care simply because one item was wrapped.</p></section>
    <section class="py-6"><h2>Build a deadline register with fulfillment</h2><p>For each destination and service, record the source of the cutoff, order-processing assumptions, timezone, excluded products and owner. Separate a merchant’s order deadline from a carrier estimate and a delivery guarantee. Link to the current store shipping policy. Hold deadline-specific copy when the destination, stock or fulfillment commitment is unresolved; this guide supplies no actual shipping dates.</p></section>
    <section class="py-6"><h2>Make the guide answer gift questions</h2><p>Group items by recipient interest, budget or required compatibility. Explain what is included and what must be chosen separately. A proposed board-game gift guide could distinguish a standalone game from an expansion that needs a base game. A tea sampler could describe contents and preparation needs without assuming the purchaser will consume it. Use real product information rather than invented customer quotations.</p></section>
    <section class="py-6"><h2>Use deadline transitions deliberately</h2><p>Choose reminders from the verified register, then check eligibility again before dispatch. Stop physical-delivery claims when their supporting cutoff expires. Offer collection or a digital gift only if the store actually provides it, with its own availability and delivery conditions. Do not describe digital fulfillment as instant without checking how issuance and review work.</p></section>
    <section class="py-6"><h2>Give the purchaser a useful handoff</h2><p>Send the buyer the relevant order and tracking information and a shareable care or setup page. Do not enroll a recipient in marketing merely because their address appears on a gift order. Recipient messaging needs a separate, reviewed enrollment decision. Link the actual gift exchange policy instead of inventing an extended holiday return window.</p></section>
    <section class="py-6"><h2>Proposed readiness review</h2><p>Rehearse a delayed dispatch, a destination change, a sold-out gift bundle, a mixed basket and an order after the cutoff. Inspect the email, linked page and support response for the same promise. Check that a refund or cancellation stops obsolete reminders. These are proposed acceptance cases; no merchant outcomes or delivery tests are claimed here.</p></section>
    <section class="py-6"><h2>Choose the tool around changing facts</h2><p>Ask Sequenzy, Klaviyo or another candidate to demonstrate gift intent storage, line-item handling, deadline replacement and cancellation of queued messages. Assign an operator who can update the deadline register and remove stale creative quickly. After the season, review incorrect gift classifications, support questions and fulfilled versus cancelled orders rather than attributing every holiday purchase to the email.</p></section>
    <p>Continue with <a class="link" href="/use-cases/discount-strategy">discount strategy</a>, <a class="link" href="/use-cases/holiday-gifting">holiday gifting</a> or the <a class="link" href="/use-cases">workflow hub</a>.</p>
    <p><a class="link" href="/apps">Explore app candidates</a> · <a class="link" href="/compare">Compare requirements</a> · <a class="link" href="/for">Store-type guides</a></p>
  </article>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Questions about this workflow" />
</BaseLayout>
